===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|interrogative}} In what way; to what extent.
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' does it matter?}}
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' do you care?}}
# {{n-g|Used before a prepositional phrase to emphasise that something is taken into consideration as a cause or reason; usually used in combination with 'with' (see [[what with]]), and much less commonly with other prepositions.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1787|author=Henry St. John; Lord Viscount Bolingbroke|title=Letters on the Study and Use of History: A Letter to Sir William Windham|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=ZtpkAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA83&dq=%22what+by%22|page=83
|passage=In short; '''what''' by the indiscretion of people here, '''what''' by the rebound which came often back from London, '''what''' by the private interests and ambitious views of persons in the French court, and '''what''' by other causes unnecessary to be examined now, the most private transactions came to light [...]}}
#* '''1815''', Rev. Mr. Milne, letter reprinted in ''The Evangelical Magazine and Missionary Chronicle, Volume 23'', page 82. [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=EPE6AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA82&dq=%22what+from%22]
#*: The Chinese of all ranks, and in every place, received my books gladly, and listened with patience to what I had to say about the true God.—So that '''what''' from opportunities of attending to the object of my Mission among the Chinese—'''what''' from seasons of religious instruction to Dutch and English—'''what''' from intercourse with gentlemen of education and knowledge of the world—'''what''' from occasions of stating clearly the object of Missions, and of endeavouring to remove prejudices against them—and '''what''' from the view of a highly cultivated country, happy under an enlightened and liberal government, I have much reason to be satisfied with this journey [...]

